EXT. ABANDONED SKATE PARK - SUNSET

Cracked concrete bowls. Graffiti. A single streetlamp flickers on. The sky is the colour of a bruise going yellow.

WILLA PRZYBYLSKI (16, buzzcut, borrowed jacket) sits on the lip of a bowl, a board across her knees. Below, MATEO ARROYO-BELL (17, tall, band-aids on his elbows) rolls a loose wheel with one finger.

WILLA
You're leaving tomorrow.

MATEO
Bus is at seven.

WILLA
Don't say goodbye.

MATEO
I wasn't going to.

WILLA
Good.

He kicks up the board and drops into the bowl. Rolls up the far wall and hangs, weightless, for one second. Comes down. She watches, lips pressed.

MATEO
Ride with me.

WILLA
I'm not wearing pads.

MATEO
Nobody's watching.

She stands and drops in. Wobbles. Recovers. They carve opposite walls, passing in the middle, never colliding.

WILLA
Don't forget me.

MATEO ^
Don't forget me.

They stop at the bottom, boards ticking, and neither speaks.
